      ],
      "text": "Procrastination\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        153.63470458984375,
        514.0982666015625,
        191.83375549316406
      ],
      "text": "A psychologist explains why we put off important tasks and how we can break\nthis habit\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        205.29205322265625,
        84.2708969116211,
        224.79345703125
      ],
      "text": "A.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        225.2747039794922,
        523.1025390625,
        403.0736083984375
      ],
      "text": "Procrastination is the habit of delaying a necessary task, usually by focusing on\nless urgent, more enjoyable, and easier activities instead. We all do it from\ntime to time. We might be composing a message to a friend who we have to\nlet down, or putting together an important report for college or work; we're\ndoing our best to avoid doing the job at hand, but deep down we know that we\nshould just be getting on with it. Unfortunately, berating ourselves won’t stop\nus procrastinating again. In fact, it’s one of the worst things we can do. This\nmatters because, as my research shows, procrastination doesn’t just waste\ntime, but is actually linked to other problems, too.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        415.89202880859375,
        83.6708984375,
        435.3934326171875
      ],
      "text": "B.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        435.9936828613281,
        523.291259765625,
        613.673583984375
      ],
      "text": "Contrary to popular belief, procrastination is not due to laziness or poor time\nmanagement. Scientific studies suggest procrastination is, in fact, caused by\npoor mood management. This makes sense if we consider that people are\nmore likely to put off starting or completing tasks that they are really not keen\nto do. If just thinking about the task threatens our sense of self-worth or makes\nus anxious, we will be more likely to put it off. Research involving brain imaging\nhas found that areas of the brain linked to detection of threats and emotion\nregulation are actually different in people who chronically procrastinate\ncompared to those who don't procrastinate frequently.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        626.6110229492188,
        83.19120025634766,
        646.1124267578125
      ],
      "text": "C.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        646.5936889648438,
        520.4619140625,
        764.7526245117188
      ],
      "text": "Tasks that are emotionally loaded or difficult, such as preparing for exams, are\nprime candidates for procrastination. People with low self-esteem are more\nlikely to procrastinate. Another group of people who tend to procrastinate are\nperfectionists, who worry their work will be judged harshly by others. We\nknow that if we don’t finish that report or complete those home repairs, then\nwhat we did can’t be evaluated. When we avoid such tasks, we also avoid the\n"
    }
